WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.850
After a scam: the first hour, reporting, recovery, and leading others.

2
00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:10.710
This is the video for module four: responding to fraud, reporting it and leading change.

3
00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:15.860
Money moved by transfer can be passed on quickly. Do not wait.

4
00:00:16.210 --> 00:00:26.240
Step one. Call the number on your ATM card. Ask them to block your card and mobile banking, contact the receiving bank, and give you a reference number.

5
00:00:27.490 --> 00:00:37.660
Step two. Uninstall any app a scammer told you to install. Change your email password, and if your line went dead, call your network to block the SIM.

6
00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:47.170
Step three. Screenshot chats, numbers, account names, amounts and dates. Download your statement. Save it all in one folder.

7
00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:52.540
Step four. Report to the police, and for financial fraud, the EFCC.

8
00:00:53.790 --> 00:01:04.300
Step five. Warn your contacts, so nobody sends money in your name. Be honest with your bank, even if you shared your OTP. Hiding it slows the investigation.

9
00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:16.500
Kemi, a hairdresser in Lagos, realised she had read out an OTP, and 95,000 naira had left her account. Two minutes later she was on the phone to her bank.

10
00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:20.610
She wrote the reference number on paper, with the time.

11
00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:28.850
She saved the call log, the OTP messages and the debit alerts, and noted both accounts the money went to.

12
00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:37.420
She reported on the NPF National Cybercrime Centre website, and planned a police station visit for a written report.

13
00:01:38.670 --> 00:01:47.460
Her sister helped her change her email password, and stayed with her. Within one hour, Kemi stopped further losses and gave herself the best chance.

14
00:01:48.710 --> 00:01:59.480
A good report often goes to more than one agency. Always start with your bank. If it does not resolve your complaint, escalate to the Central Bank, with your reference number.

15
00:02:00.730 --> 00:02:09.140
Report to the police. Banks often ask for a police report. For financial fraud, report to the EFCC on its official website.

16
00:02:10.390 --> 00:02:25.190
For SIM swap or scam calls, complain to your network first. If unresolved, call the NCC on 622. Report fake pages on the platform. Investment schemes go to SEC Nigeria.

17
00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:38.110
Musa lost 180,000 naira to a fake phone shop in Abuja. His report had all six parts, and he sent the same clear facts to his bank, the police and the EFCC.

18
00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:44.590
Never pay anyone who offers to get your money back for a fee. That is almost always a second scam.

19
00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:52.810
Shock, shame, anger and fear are normal after fraud. Many victims say the shame was harder than the money.

20
00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:59.310
The scammer is responsible for the crime. You are responsible only for what you do next.

21
00:02:59.660 --> 00:03:11.090
If, weeks later, you still cannot sleep, eat or work, or you feel hopeless, please talk to a doctor, a counsellor or a trusted religious leader. You do not have to carry it alone.

22
00:03:12.340 --> 00:03:20.730
Bisi reported the scheme to the EFCC and SEC Nigeria, and told her landlord early. He agreed she could pay rent in two parts.

23
00:03:21.980 --> 00:03:34.840
She lost 400,000 naira. She cut spending and saved 40,000 a month in an account with no card. After four months she had 160,000, enough for the first part of the rent.

24
00:03:35.190 --> 00:03:42.190
She avoided quick-fix loans and new investment offers, because desperation makes people open to a second scam.

25
00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:48.500
You now know more than most people around you. Start by letting two people share a story.

26
00:03:49.750 --> 00:03:56.290
Teach the three parts of a scam, and the golden rule: no PIN, OTP or BVN to anyone.

27
00:03:57.540 --> 00:04:03.110
Role-play is the most useful part. People rehearse saying, I don't do codes on the phone.

28
00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:08.220
Show how to check a balance in the app instead of trusting an SMS.

29
00:04:09.470 --> 00:04:19.710
Rukayat ran this for 25 traders in Ibadan. A month later, eighteen said they now check their app every time. Share patterns, not victims' names.

30
00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:29.820
To remember. Call your bank first, at once. Save evidence before you block. Report to the right agencies, and never pay to recover your money.

31
00:04:30.170 --> 00:04:34.180
Be kind to yourself. Then turn your knowledge into protection for others.

32
00:04:35.430 --> 00:04:44.750
Now practise. Make your first-hour card, draft a practice report, write a recovery plan, and plan a 30-minute session for a group you belong to.

33
00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:47.040
Continue with the lessons below.
